scope:
This file consists of a criminal case filed in 1943. The defendants and the unindicted co-conspirators were accused of violating Section 34, Title 50, USC, (Act of June 15, 1917, Chapter 30, Title 1, Section 2; 40 Stat. 218) also known as the Espionage Act of 1917. The indictment claimed that the accused “unlawfully, wilfully and feloniously did combine, conspire, confederate and agree together, one with the other and each with the other, and with the government of the German Reich” to pass to Nazi Germany “documents, writings, code books, signal books, sketches, photographs, photographic negatives, blueprints, plans, maps, models, notes, instruments, appliances and information relating to the national defense of the United States." John S. Bugas, Special Agent of the Federal Bureau of Investigation, and future Vice President of the Ford Motor Company investigated the case and Edward J. Moinet presided. 

